course: dictionary
lesson: 9623
title: Stubble, one word with two places
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Read the words around them to find out which meaning fits. «Dad's stubble scratched my cheek when he hugged me» tells you the word is about a face, so stubble means the short, rough hairs that grow when a man has not shaved for a day or two.
explain: The same word can mean something different in another place. «After the wheat was cut, the tractor ploughed through the stubble» is about a field, so here stubble means the short, stiff stalks left standing after a crop has been harvested.
explain: Ask yourself: where am I, a face or a field? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check it still makes sense. Careful with myths too. «Stubble feels rough, so "shaving makes hair thicker"» is a myth, a false belief, because feeling rough does not mean the hair has changed.
fact: Stubble means short, rough hairs on a man's face after he has not shaved for a day or a few days.
fact: Stubble also means the short, stiff stalks left in a field after a crop like wheat has been cut.
fact: Stubble is uncountable, so we say some stubble, not a stubble.
fact: «A bit of stubble» means a small amount of hair on the chin.
fact: «Designer stubble» means stubble kept neat on purpose, not left alone.
exercise: «Dad's stubble scratched my cheek.» What is stubble here? | *Short, rough hairs on a face | A soft, smooth beard | Dry leaves on a path | Tall grass in a meadow
exercise: «After the harvest, the farmer ploughed the stubble back into the field.» Where is the stubble? | *In a field, as short stiff stalks | In a bag of seeds | On a man's chin | In a shed
exercise: Is stubble countable or uncountable? | *Uncountable, so we say some stubble | Countable, so we say three stubbles | It is a verb, like run | Only used in the plural
test: Mum says, «You need a shave, you've got stubble.» What does she mean? | Your face is covered in mud | *Hairs have grown on your face since you last shaved | You are going to a party | You have a cold
test: «Stubble feels rough, so shaving makes hair thicker» is a myth. Why? | Shaving makes hair grow faster and thicker | Stubble is always blue | *Stubble only feels rough; shaving does not change how hair grows | It is true, because shaving makes hair grow thicker
exam: Which sentence uses stubble to mean the stalks left in a field? | Dad skipped his shave, so stubble covered his chin. | *After the wheat was cut, only stubble was left in the field. | She trimmed the stubble on her husband's chin. | His cheek felt scratchy from the stubble.
